    {
      "year": 150,
      "yearLabel": "AD 150",
      "name": "Roma, caput mundi",
      "dossier": {
        "population": null,
        "populationLabel": "450,000 to 1,000,000",
        "populationConfidence": "approximate",
        "ruler": "Antoninus Pius, Roman Emperor",
        "story": "The city at its height, and famously without a wall: no enemy has come near it in centuries and none is expected. The Colosseum has stood for seventy years, Trajan's column for nearly forty, and Hadrian's Pantheon is a generation old. How many people lived here is genuinely disputed — estimates run from about 450,000 to over a million, and the argument turns on how densely an ancient city could be packed.",
        "seeing": "the Colosseum east of the Forum, the Pantheon on the flat ground by the river, Trajan's column, the Circus Maximus in the valley between the Palatine and the Aventine, and an aqueduct striding in from the hills. No city wall.",
